Title: The Honk
Order: 6
Production Code: 1x06

Cast: JIM DAVIS, JULIA, FRED, J.B.H. (JULIA'S FATHER), THE CROWD, JIM'S WIFE, THE NARRATOR. Running time target: 10-13 minutes.

CONTENT NOTE: This episode keeps the source's racial slur exactly as Du Bois wrote it, in the mouths of Fred and the crowd. See the README and world/notes/how-to-deliver-the-slur.md for the production question. Do not bleep, soften, or cover it with sound.

PRODUCTION NOTE: Sound returns in a rush. The city's whole soundscape comes back one layer at a time (motor, rocket, elevators, voices, lights), each bringing the old world's weight. The drama ends on the Hymn, spoken as written.

EXT. THE ROOF OF THE METROPOLITAN TOWER - NIGHT - CONTINUOUS

AMB: the roof, the night, the wind; the sky's shimmer still in the air

SFX: from below, close, sudden and ugly in all that stillness: a motor horn - HONK. HONK.

NARRATOR
"Honk! Honk!" Hoarse and sharp, the cry of a motor drifted clearly up from the silence below.

SFX: Julia gasps; Jim steps back; his heel scrapes the roof

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
They started backward with a cry, and gazed upon each other with eyes that faltered and fell, with blood that boiled.

MUSIC: STING - a harsh, flat brass note, instantly cut off

SFX: HONK. HONK. HONK. HONK. - mad, rapid, the same horn

SFX: from somewhere almost at their feet, a rocket blazes into the air; a hiss, a shriek, a burst; it scatters stars upon them

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
And almost from their feet a rocket blazed into the air, and scattered its stars upon them.

SFX: Julia covers her face; a muffled sob; her shoulders heave

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
She covered her eyes with her hands, and her shoulders heaved. He dropped, and bowed, groped blindly on his knees about the floor.

SFX: his hands on tar paper; the clatter of matchboxes; a match scratching, failing, scratching, failing

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
A blue flame spluttered lazily after an age, and she heard the scream of an answering rocket as it flew.

SFX: a rocket fuse; a hiss; a shriek as it goes up and bursts

A long beat. Wind. They do not look at each other.

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
Then they stood still as death, looking to opposite ends of the earth.

MUSIC: BED IN - a thin string tone, held; neither major nor minor

SFX: far below, a clang

SFX: "Clang - crash - clang!" - the roar and ring of swift elevators shooting up from below; the whole tower begins to tremble

MUSIC: BED OUT

NARRATOR
The roar and ring of swift elevators shooting upward from below made the great tower tremble.

AMB: the tower's walls, humming with the elevators' weight

SFX: a babel of voices swelling from the stairwell; shouting, laughter, orders, calls

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
A murmur and babel of voices swept in upon the night.

SFX: across the whole city, light switching on - a hundred power stations coming back; a rolling hum, a faint buzz, a flicker of arc lamps; a distant roar like a stadium

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
All over the once dead city the lights blinked, flickered, and flamed.

SFX: elevator doors crashing open; the sound of a hundred men on the platform, boots on tar paper

AMB: a crowd - the whole roof filled with talk, the world back in a rush

Voices all together, overlapping, close and urgent, no single word clear.

SFX: a heavy tread, fast; a man's voice, hoarse, breaking

J.B.H.
(sobbing)
My daughter!

SFX: a rush of steps; Julia lifted off her feet into a tight embrace; her cry muffled in his coat

NARRATOR
And one with white and flying hair rushed to the girl, and lifted her to his breast.

SFX: a second set of steps, light and quick; leather gloves

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
Behind him hurried a younger, comelier man, carefully clad in motor costume, who bent above the girl with passionate solicitude.

FRED
(a whisper, hot, urgent)
Julia. My darling. I thought you were gone forever.

SFX: her breath, in quick shallow draws; she looks up; a rustle of silk

JULIA
(almost vaguely)
Fred. Is the world gone?

FRED
(quickly)
Only New York.

SFX: his hands on her shoulders; her silk crushed

FRED (CONT'D)
(rushing)
It is terrible, awful! You know - but you - how did you escape? How have you endured this horror? Are you well? Unharmed?

JULIA
(flatly)
Unharmed!

FRED
(his arm round her, turning)
And this man here?

SFX: a boot heel on tar paper; the leather of his glove; a hand moving to a hip

MUSIC: STING - a low, hard brass note

FRED (CONT'D)
(a snarl)
Why! It's - a - nigger - Julia! Has he - has he dared -

SFX: the crowd's murmur falls; the tower's wind returns

A long beat. Julia lifts her head and looks at Jim; drops her eyes; a long breath.

JULIA
(quietly)
He has dared - all - to rescue me. And I - thank him - much.

NARRATOR
But she did not look at him again.

SFX: Julia's steps, and Fred's; Julia and Fred leaving; steps fading toward the elevators

SFX: a hand in a coat pocket; paper, a roll of bills drawn out; peeled flat

J.B.H.
(thrusting it into the man's hands)
Here, my good fellow - take that. What's your name?

SFX: Jim's hand closing, slowly, on the money; his breath

JIM DAVIS
(hollow-voiced)
Jim Davis.

J.B.H.
(booming, genial)
Well, Jim, I thank you. I've always liked your people. If you ever want a job, call on me.

SFX: his steps going; a gate of an elevator; the cab's door; they are gone

A beat. The crowd fills the silence.

NARRATOR
And they were gone.

SFX: a crowd pouring up and out of the elevators; whispering; shoes; the babel of a hundred men

THE CROWD
(overlapping)
Who was it?

THE CROWD (CONT'D)
(another voice)
Are they alive?

THE CROWD (CONT'D)
(another)
How many?

THE CROWD (CONT'D)
(another, shouting)
Two!

THE CROWD (CONT'D)
(another)
Who was saved?

THE CROWD (CONT'D)
(another, a laugh in it)
A white girl and a nigger - there she goes.

THE CROWD (CONT'D)
(another voice, rising)
A nigger? Where is he? Let's lynch the damned -

THE CROWD (CONT'D)
(another voice, flat, tired)
Shut up. He's all right - he saved her.

THE CROWD (CONT'D)
(the first)
Saved hell! He had no business -

THE CROWD (CONT'D)
(another)
Here he comes.

SFX: the crowd parting; a hundred feet stepping aside

MUSIC: BED IN - low, slow strings, the first the roof has heard since the soprano; one voice, mournful

SFX: electric light - a great arc lamp flaring on; a buzz; Jim's steps

NARRATOR
Into the glare of the electric lights, the colored man moved slowly, with the eyes of those that walk and sleep.

THE CROWD (CONT'D)
(a bystander, amazed)
Well, what do you think of that? Of all New York - just a white girl and a nigger!

SFX: Jim does not respond; no change in his breath

NARRATOR
The colored man heard nothing. He stood silently beneath the glare of the light, gazing at the money in his hand, and shrinking as he gazed.

SFX: the crumple of paper money; his fingers curling and uncurling

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
Slowly he put his other hand into his pocket, and brought out a baby's filmy cap, and gazed again.

SFX: a small bit of cloth, the softest sound in the episode; unfolded; his thumb passing over it

MUSIC: BED OUT - held, fading

A hush on the platform. A heel on tar paper, small, uneven, someone climbing.

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
A woman mounted to the platform, and looked about, shading her eyes. She was brown, small, and toil-worn, and in one arm lay the corpse of a dark baby.

SFX: a woman's step, labored, unsteady; the faint, soft knock of a head on a shoulder; breathing hard from the stairs

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
The crowd parted, and her eyes fell on the colored man. With a cry, she tottered toward him.

JIM'S WIFE
(a cry, a whole life in one word)
Jim!

SFX: the money falling from his hand; scattering over the roof in the wind

NARRATOR
He whirled, and, with a sob of joy, caught her in his arms.

MUSIC: SWELL - warm strings, quiet; the first sound in the series that is simply, plainly home

SFX: a long, held embrace; a baby's blanket, cloth rustling; quiet weeping

A long moment of the crowd, the lights, the wind. The city is back. This is the only thing in it that matters.

MUSIC: OUT

EXT. THE ROOF - THE SKY - FADE OUT

AMB: the city's night, now alive: far-off horns, a train, a shout, a dog, a distant bell. The comet in the west.

MUSIC: BED IN - a solo cello, then low voices, unaccompanied, singing no words at first, just a long, slow line; the Hymn's setting

NARRATOR
A Hymn to the Peoples.

A beat. The voices below are fading. The Narrator reads the Hymn in full, slowly, over the choral bed, as Du Bois wrote it.

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
O Truce of God! And primal meeting of the Sons of Man, foreshadowing the union of the World! From all the ends of earth we come!

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
Old Night, the elder sister of the Day, Mother of Dawn in the golden East, meets in the misty twilight with her brood, pale and black, tawny, red and brown, the mighty human rainbow of the world, spanning its wilderness of storm.

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
Softly in sympathy the sunlight falls, rare is the radiance of the moon; and on the darkest midnight blaze the stars - the far-flown shadows of whose brilliance drop like a dream on the dim shores of Time, forecasting Days that are to these as day to night.

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
So sit we all as one. So, gloomed in tall and stone-swathed groves, the Buddha walks with Christ! And Al-Koran and Bible both be holy!

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
Almighty Word! In this Thine awful sanctuary, First and flame-haunted City of the Widened World, assoil us, Lord of Lands and Seas!

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
We are but weak and wayward men, distraught alike with hatred and vainglory; prone to despise the Soul that breathes within - high visioned hordes that lie and steal and kill, sinning the sin each separate heart disclaims, clambering upon our riven, writhing selves, besieging Heaven by trampling men to Hell! We be blood-guilty! Lo, our hands be red! Not one may blame the other in this sin!

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
But here - here in the white Silence of the Dawn, before the Womb of Time, with bowed hearts all flame and shame, we face the birth-pangs of a world: we hear the stifled cry of Nations all but born - the wail of women ravished of their stunted brood! We see the nakedness of Toil, the poverty of Wealth, we know the Anarchy of Empire, and doleful Death of Life! And hearing, seeing, knowing all, we cry:

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
Save us, World-Spirit, from our lesser selves! Grant us that war and hatred cease, reveal our souls in every race and hue! Help us, O Human God, in this Thy Truce, to make Humanity divine!

MUSIC: SWELL - the choir opens on the last line, then holds

AMB: the city's night, soft, going quiet

MUSIC: OUT - a single voice, then silence

SFX: one last, far-off horn

AMB: silence. Hold for five seconds, and out.
